\subsection{Computational Complexity and Hardware Feasibility}
We profile hardware execution on an ARM Cortex-M4/A MCU clocked at 168~MHz (`hardware_feasibility.csv`), as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:hardware_profile} and Table~\ref{tab:hardware_stats}. REMO-DQN requires 3.8M MACs, 350K parameters, and 1.4~MB memory footprint. Forward inference completes in \textbf{1.2~ms}, consuming only \textbf{1.2\% of the 100~ms DCC interval}, leaving 98.8~ms of MCU headroom for critical vehicle safety tasks.

\subsection{Ablation Study and MoE Domain Specialization}
\subsubsection{Structural Ablation Study}
Fig.~\ref{fig:ablation} and Table~\ref{tab:ablation_stats} evaluate structural ablations (`ablation_study.csv`). Monolithic Vanilla DQN suffers high density collapse (1.21\% PDR, AoI 1\,290.89~ms). Adding MoE routing (`DQN+MoE`) improves PDR to 65.20\% and reduces CBR standard deviation to 0.1058. Integrating the ResNet backbone and Dueling streams (`REMO-DQN`) elevates mean PDR to \textbf{75.02\%} (73.41\% at high density), minimizes CBR standard deviation to \textbf{0.1008}, and reduces AoI to \textbf{373.21~ms}, verifying the synergy of all components.

